+{"question_index": 20, "question_id": 11319, "category": "engineering", "k": 0, "gold": "H", "parsed_letter": "A", "correct": false, "full_text": "*   Power (P) = 2,500 kW = 2,500,000 W\n    *   Voltage (V) = 600 V\n    *   Number of poles (P) = 16\n    *   Armature winding type: Lap-wound\n    *   Total number of armature conductors (Z) = 2,360\n    *   Pole faces cover 65% of the circumference.\n    *   Objective: Find the number of pole-face conductors in each pole of a *compensating winding*.\n\n    *   In a lap winding, the number of armature conductors per pole (Z/P) is related to the winding.\n    *   The number of armature conductors per pole is $Z/P = 2,360 / 16 = 147.5$. (Wait, let's re-check the standard formula).\n    *   Actually, in a lap winding, the number of armature conductors per pole is $Z/P$.\n    *   $Z/P = 2,360 / 16 = 147.5$.\n\n    *   The number of armature conductors in each pole is $Z/P$.\n    *   The question asks for the number of pole-face conductors in each pole of a *compensating winding*.\n    *   A compensating winding is used to neutralize the armature reaction.\n    *   The number of conductors in the compensating winding is typically related to the number of armature conductors in each pole.\n    *   Wait, let me re-read the question carefully: \"calculate the number of pole-face conductors in each pole of a compensating winding.\"\n\n    *   The number of armature conductors per pole is $Z/P = 2,360 / 16 = 147.5$.\n    *   The pole faces cover 65% of the circumference.\n    *   The number of armature conductors *on* the pole faces would be (number of conductors per pole) * (percentage of circumference covered).\n    *   Number of armature conductors on pole faces = $(Z/P) \\times 0.65 = 147.5 \\times 0.65 = 95.875$.\n    *\n"}
